[THOUGHT]  
The clinical picture remains highly consistent with **Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo (BPPV)**, specifically involving the right side. Key supportive features include:  
- Classic *positional* vertigo (spinning, false motion) triggered by head movement (rolling over, looking up);  
- Absence of auditory symptoms (no hearing loss, tinnitus, ear fullness) or central neurologic red flags;  
- Positive Dix-Hallpike maneuver with *right-sided horizontal nystagmus* — though “horizontal” is atypical for classic posterior canal BPPV (which usually shows *upbeating torsional* nystagmus), it raises consideration of either:  
  • Horizontal canal BPPV (canalolithiasis or cupulolithiasis), where geotropic or ageotropic horizontal nystagmus is expected on supine roll test — but *supine roll test findings are not reported*;  
  • Atypical posterior canal BPPV (e.g., light cupulolithiasis or variant anatomy) producing predominantly horizontal nystagmus;  
  • Or a documentation limitation — the term “horizontal” may reflect gross observation without detailed plane analysis (e.g., missing subtle torsional component).  

Critically, multiple requested nystagmus descriptors — **plane (beyond “horizontal”), direction (geotropic/ageotropic, torsional orientation), latency, duration, fatigability, and habituation** — are *not specified* in any measurement or patient report. Per protocol, these must be explicitly stated to be included; absence = “Not specified”. No contradictory data (e.g., prolonged duration, non-fatigable nystagmus, spontaneous vertigo) challenges the BPPV diagnosis.  

[SUMMARY]  
**Diagnosis:** Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo (BPPV), right-sided — most likely involving the posterior or horizontal semicircular canal. Current evidence supports BPPV but does *not* permit definitive canal localization due to incomplete nystagmus characterization.

**Key Confirmed Findings:**  
- Symptom profile: Positional vertigo (spinning, motion illusion), provoked by head movement (e.g., rolling over, looking up).  
- No associated auditory or central neurologic symptoms.  
- Positive Dix-Hallpike maneuver: Right-sided *horizontal* nystagmus upon sitting-to-supine transition.  
- VNG confirms abnormal positional response isolated to Dix-Hallpike — consistent with peripheral canalithiasis.

**Critical Gaps (Per Protocol):**  
All of the following are **Not specified**:  
- Nystagmus plane beyond “horizontal” (e.g., presence/absence of torsional component);  
- Direction of horizontal nystagmus during supine roll test (geotropic vs. ageotropic);  
- Torsional direction during Dix-Hallpike (e.g., top poles beating toward lower ear);  
- Latency (time from position change to nystagmus onset);  
- Duration (in seconds) of nystagmus/vertigo;  
- Fatigability (reduction with repetition);  
- Habituation (decrease in intensity over serial testing).

**Clinical Implication:**  
Definitive canal assignment (posterior vs. horizontal) requires completion of:  
- Supine roll test with video-Frenzel or VNG documentation of geotropic/ageotropic horizontal nystagmus;  
- Repeat Dix-Hallpike with real-time characterization of nystagmus plane (e.g., torsional-horizontal), direction, latency, and fatigability.  
Until then, empiric repositioning (e.g., Epley for suspected posterior canal; barbecue roll for horizontal canal) may be considered based on predominant symptom triggers and initial maneuver response.

